            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Verdi's Innovations in Opera</strong><br>\nVerdi is credited with expanding the musical language of opera, emphasizing dramatic expression, and integrating theater with music more closely than ever before, paving the way for future composers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Retirement</strong><br>\nVerdi's personal life in his later years was quieter, as he spent time at his estate in Sant'Agata with his second wife, Giuseppina Strepponi, a renowned opera singer who had premiered several of his works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nVerdi died in 1901, leaving behind a body of work that secured his place as one of the greatest opera composers in history. His operas remain staples of the repertoire worldwide, celebrated for their emotional power and melodic beauty.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
